Title: IAM (Identity & Access Management).
Description: Put in some study time for a deeper understanding IAM
Author: Rodrigo Aurelio Criscuolo
MS. Author: RodrigoACriscuolo
MS. Date 10/09/2026

Abstract:
   Identity and Access Management (IAM) is the method of identifying users, devices, applications, services, and other digital entities, then controlling what each entity may access and what actions it may perform. In simple terms, IAM answers four questions:
   1 - Who or what is requesting access?
   2 - Can the identity be verified?
   3 - What resource may it access?
   4 - What actions are allowed, under which conditions, and for how long?

   So, IAM combines identity administration, authentication, authorization, access control, monitoring and auditing.
   Microsoft summarizes the objective as ensuring that the right people, machines, and software access the right resources at the right time. Google gives expresses the same idea as controlling who can do what on which resources.
Links:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/fundamentals/identity-fundamental-concepts
https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview

Keywords:


1. Introduction

Nowadays companies no longer operate from one protected office network. Their users, applications, data, devices, cloud services, APIs, contractors, customers, and AI agents may be are distributed across many locations and platforms.
Without IAM, organiztions commonly experience questions like:
   * Excessive user privileges
   * Shared accounts and passwords
   * Former employees retaining access
   * Poor visibility into who accessed sensitive data
   * Uncontrolled service accounts and API keys, or those mistakenly believed to be under control.
   * Difficult Onboarding and Offboarding
   * Increased damage after a compromised account
   * Weak evidence for audits and regulatory compliance

The central question is therefore:
   1 - How can an organization provide lefitimate access efficiently while reducing unathorized access and excessive privilege? IAM is one of the primary technical and organizational responses to this problem.
   2 - But would that be enough?

